          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"undset-kristin-lavransdatter-85c5da838a1e222d\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Erlend directly avows that he loved Kristin even while treating her badly as well as when treating her well. The utterance primarily presents the constancy of his loving state as true. Although it forms part of his attempt to persuade her to remain with him, the love clause itself does not undertake a commitment, and nothing presents it as a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The surrounding plea for Kristin to stay gives the avowal a relationship-repairing purpose, but that persuasive function does not independently establish performative undertaking force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Erlend directly says to Kristin, “Men jeg elsket dig” (“But I loved you”), in face-to-face dialogue.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Erlend is urging Kristin to remain with him at Haugen. After acknowledging that their life together turned out painfully and that he failed her, he insists that his love persisted whether he acted badly or well. The past-tense avowal is reinforced by his immediately preceding claim that he loves her as dearly today as when they were young. Its rhetorical purpose is reassurance and persuasion; the separate imperative “Bli her, Kristin” carries the explicit appeal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Men jeg elsket dig, naar jeg handlet ilde med dig og naar jeg handlet vel mot dig.”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit clause asserts that Erlend's love remained present across both his bad and good treatment of Kristin, strongly supporting truth-conditional avowal.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before the target, Erlend says, “jeg har dig saa kjær idag som den kvelden du sov indunder kappen min.”\",\"supports\":\"His assertion that Kristin is as dear to him today as in their youth frames the target as a sincere-seeming claim of continuous love rather than merely a historical report.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The target is followed by the separate plea, “Bli her, Kristin —!”\",\"supports\":\"The dialogue has a persuasive, relationship-repairing context, but the explicit request is linguistically separate from the love avowal; this limits support for P in the core utterance.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Erlend first acknowledges that events turned out differently from what he had meant and that this is how life often goes.\",\"supports\":\"The avowal answers the history of harm by distinguishing his enduring emotional state from his conduct, reinforcing T rather than E or P.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T gives a natural and sufficient account of the core utterance as an avowal of enduring love. Its persuasive setting and emotional intensity can be represented contextually without adding P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The supplied translation aid concerns a different nearby love formulation involving Simon; the classification relies on the Norwegian target and its immediate source context.\"}"
        }
      ],
